Here’s a clear **Promise chaining example** in JavaScript that you can use in interviews or projects:

```js
// Example: Fetch user, then fetch their posts, then log results
function getUser(id) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve({ id, name: "Ashritha" }), 1000);
  });
}

function getPosts(userId) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve([
      { id: 1, title: "Post 1", userId },
      { id: 2, title: "Post 2", userId }
    ]), 1000);
  });
}

function getComments(postId) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve([
      { id: 101, text: "Nice post!", postId },
      { id: 102, text: "Thanks for sharing!", postId }
    ]), 1000);
  });
}

// Promise chaining
getUser(1)
  .then(user => {
    console.log("User:", user);
    return getPosts(user.id); // chain next promise
  })
  .then(posts => {
    console.log("Posts:", posts);
    return getComments(posts[0].id); // chain another promise
  })
  .then(comments => {
    console.log("Comments on first post:", comments);
  })
  .catch(err => {
    console.error("Error:", err);
  });
```

---

## 🔎 What’s Happening
1. `getUser(1)` resolves with a user object.  
2. `.then()` passes that user to `getPosts(user.id)`.  
3. Next `.then()` passes posts to `getComments(posts[0].id)`.  
4. Finally, comments are logged.  
5. Any error in the chain is caught by `.catch()`.

---

## 🎯 Interview Tip
If asked this in an interview, you can say:

> “I handle asynchronous flows in JavaScript using Promise chaining when tasks depend on each other sequentially. Each `.then()` returns a new Promise, allowing me to pass results down the chain. For cleaner syntax, I often use `async/await`, but Promise chaining is useful to demonstrate explicit control of execution order.”

